## Bug Identification

The problem is on the `if` condition line:

```javascript
if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false) {
```

An empty string `""` is a valid, non-null, non-undefined, non-false value. It passes all three checks, so the function returns `true`. The condition never checks whether the string is actually non-empty.

## Why it happens

In JavaScript, `""` is a distinct primitive value. It is:
- not `null` → `"" !== null` is `true`
- not `undefined` → `"" !== undefined` is `true`
- not `false` → `"" !== false` is `true`

All conditions pass, so `validateInput("")` falls into the `return true` branch.

## Fix

Add a check that the input is not an empty string:

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
</solution>

This is the minimal change — a single additional `&& input !== ""` clause — that causes the empty string to fall through to `return false`, matching the intended behavior.

