## The Bug

The goroutine closures capture the loop variable `i` **by reference**, not by value. All five goroutines share the *same* variable `i`. By the time any goroutine actually runs, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to `5`. So every goroutine prints `5`.

This is the classic Go loop-variable capture pitfall. (In Go 1.22+, the loop variable is per-iteration by default, which eliminates this bug. But you're on Go 1.21, where the old semantics still apply.)

## The Fix

Capture the current value of `i` by passing it as an argument to the anonymous function:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

This creates a new local `i` for each goroutine, scoped to that particular invocation.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

